.com> jdg00@ccc.amdahl.com (Josh Grosse) writes:

   >... The only "official" ports of Nethack are for:

   >     MS-DOS
   >     OS/2
   >     Macintosh
   >     Atari
   >     Amiga

   That's it.  We've got some basic terminology differences.

   My understanding of a "ported" product:  binary and supported.

No. A "port" means to move code and modify it so it runs.
The default setup for nethack is SunOS now, it used to be
Ultrix. NetHack has always come from UNIX, and ALL OTHER
systems are referred to as "ports."

BINARIES are only available for the PC-type computers, but that's
another matter.

   This comes from my understanding of the commercial Open Systems
   marketplace, where 3rd party software vendors "port" their products
   to various Unix platforms.  I'm certainly willing to adjust the this
   definition to meet the needs of the FAQ.

And the rest of the world :-) The "porting" process is the
actual work of modifying code so it works on a new platform.
And, yes, VMS is a supported port.

   I assumed 1) that a "port" would be pre-generated, and 2) if it isn't stored at
   linc.cis.upenn.edu, it isn't official.

Well, the code is what's official. The binaries are usually
built by the team members of the various port teams, but that's
no guarantee :-)

The most official you can get is to lift the code from linc and
build the binaries yourself.

   Unless these variants are all available for distribution and supported,
   I can't see calling them "official".

They are supported in the code using #defines. (At least many of
them still are) For instance, A/UX from Apple runs NetHack out of
the box, but to use some funky things like job control you have to
hack a little (not much) because of the strange BSD/ATT mix.

   Would everyone be happy if I updated the FAQ to mention the official source
   variants, the official binary variants, and the one unofficial VMS binary?

Yes. NetHack is still a "source" game, I believe we can view the
binaries as a "public service" from the dev team.

